"""Preparation-only reference solution for ``foreach_trunc`` (operator 1869).

This file stands in for the candidate during the project
``--phase reference-as-solution`` review.  It is installed through the frozen
FlagGems override path -- ``--override foreach_trunc:<abs path to this
file>:run`` -- which ``src/flag_gems/cli_override.py:137-185`` parses and
``src/flag_gems/dynamic_registry.py:260-301`` applies with ``setattr`` on the
``flag_gems`` module.  That is exactly the attribute the frozen suites use:

* accuracy callsite ``flag_gems.foreach_trunc(...)``
  (tests/test_foreach_trunc.py:26-27,98,113,125,143);
* benchmark callsite ``op_name="foreach_trunc"`` resolved by
  ``Config.override_registry.get_override(self.op_name)``
  (benchmark/test_foreach_trunc.py:50, benchmark/base.py:621-642).

``--candidate`` in the probe must name this same file (real path) so that its
content hash matches ``--reference-source``; the recorded override path must
also resolve to the same file, because the probe identifies candidate frames by
file and by the ``run`` entrypoint name.

Oracle: the native ATen operator the frozen suites themselves use as their
reference, ``torch.ops.aten._foreach_trunc`` (tests/test_foreach_trunc.py:23,
97,112,123; benchmark/test_foreach_trunc.py:22).  Delegating to it preserves
the complete semantics asserted by the frozen cases:

* one freshly allocated output per input, in input order, with the input
  dtype, shape, layout strides and device; the frozen accuracy case also
  asserts a different ``untyped_storage().data_ptr()`` from its input;
* integral inputs are value/dtype identity (``same_dtype`` policy: int16 /
  int32 / int64 stay integral, no float promotion);
* noncontiguous inputs are read through their real strides, so the frozen
  transposed case is compared on the same values as the reference;
* ATen's device-independent foreach preconditions: an empty tensor list raises
  ``RuntimeError("Tensor list must have at least one tensor.")`` and bool /
  complex dtypes raise ``NotImplementedError`` (a ``RuntimeError`` subclass),
  which is what the frozen ``pytest.raises(RuntimeError)`` cases cover.  The
  same restrictions for this operator are documented against the ATen
  reference in the master backend implementation
  (src/flag_gems/runtime/backend/_metax/ops/foreach_trunc.py:17-23,470-471);
* no input list element is mutated (Definition effects ``mutates: []``,
  ``returns_alias_of: {}``), so the frozen benchmark may reuse one input tuple
  for the reference timing and the candidate timing
  (benchmark/base.py:870-899).

This module never imports ``flag_gems`` and never calls the candidate
attribute it replaces, so it is not its own oracle and cannot re-enter the
overridden dispatch: ``torch.ops.aten._foreach_trunc`` is only rebound to
FlagGems kernels inside ``flag_gems.use_gems()`` / ``enable()``
(src/flag_gems/__init__.py:1876-1920), and neither reviewed phase enters that
scope while an override is active (``benchmark/base.py:_candidate_call``
returns a ``nullcontext`` dispatch for an injected override, and the accuracy
tests call the wrapper directly, never under ``use_gems``).
"""

import torch

__all__ = ["run"]

_ATEN_FOREACH_TRUNC = torch.ops.aten._foreach_trunc


def run(values):
    """``aten::_foreach_trunc(Tensor[] self) -> Tensor[]``."""
    return _ATEN_FOREACH_TRUNC(values)
